Text BEFORE:
You called `reserve`, not `resize`, so your `vector` isn't actually increased in size (actual available indices), just capacity (pre-allocated memory that avoids reallocations if you don't exceed the reserved capacity). But that makes all your indexing assignments illegal; the `vector` is empty, you can't assign to *any* index. It happens to work because the `reserve` ensures there *is* memory there, but said memory would never be properly destructed (so it's a good thing you're using primitive types without destructors).

Change your loop to:

        for (int i=0; i<n; ++i){            
            // populate arr x val
            temp.push_back(nums[i]);
            // populate arr y val
            temp.push_back(nums[i+n]);
        }

pushing new values onto the end, and saving a couple unnecessary variables, rather than indexing.

